    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm running some organic kale in the PNW and noticed a significant nitrogen deficiency in my latest growth cycle. My soil pH is sitting at 6.2 and I've been applying compost regularly, but it hasn't helped much. I'm trying to decide between using bone meal or blood meal to bump up the nitrogen levels without causing a major spike in the pH levels. Which one would you recommend for this specific situation?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Considering your soil pH and the need to avoid significant pH spikes, I would recommend using bone meal over blood meal. Bone meal generally has a lower nitrogen content (around 5-7%) compared to blood meal (usually around 9-11%), which makes it a more controlled and less pH-altering choice. Additionally, bone meal's slower release of nutrients can be beneficial for kale's growth, as it provides a steady supply of nitrogen over time.",
